    "- **Vacuum weekly with a sealed HEPA machine.** An unsealed vacuum can pass fine allergen straight back into the room through the exhaust, which briefly makes things worse.",
    "> **Dealing with more than mites? See how to reduce indoor allergens naturally.** [Learn more →](/blog/how-to-reduce-indoor-allergens-naturally)",
    "## What doesn't work (or barely does)",
    "Most dust mite advice goes wrong on the methods below, so check each one before you spend money on it.",
    "| Method | Verdict |\n| :---- | :---- |\n| **Air purifiers (HEPA)** | Minimal effect on mites specifically. Mites don't fly and the allergen settles within minutes. A HEPA unit is worth having for pollen, dander, and smoke, but it does not solve dust mites. |\n| **Essential oils and \"natural sprays\"** | No reliable evidence at household concentrations. Tea tree and eucalyptus appear in lab studies at strengths you would not use on a mattress. |\n| **Freezing alone** | Kills mites but leaves the allergen in place. Must be followed by washing. |\n| **Sunlight and \"airing out\" alone** | Helps by drying the material, which is useful, but does not clear an established reservoir. |\n| **Steam cleaning carpet** | Can kill mites, but adds moisture, which is counterproductive unless you dry the surface thoroughly and quickly. |\n| **Anti-mite chemical sprays (acaricides)** | Mixed evidence, and they add chemistry to the surface you breathe against for eight hours a night. Most guidance favours physical measures first. |\n| **Ordinary vacuuming** | Removes surface debris but does not extract mites embedded deep in a mattress. Useful as maintenance only. |",
    "## Why air purifiers barely help, and what does address the surface",
    "Manufacturers market the entire air purifier category against \"dust,\" and you could reasonably assume that includes dust mites.",
    "A HEPA purifier pulls air through a filter and traps what passes through. That mechanism requires the allergen to be in the air and moving toward the unit, and dust mite allergen is in your mattress. When you disturb it, the particles are relatively heavy and settle back down within minutes, usually long before they cross the room to a filter. The purifier works as designed, but the allergen never reaches it. We break down the general mechanism difference in [probiotic vs HEPA](/blog/probiotic-vs-hepa-air-purifier), but dust mites are the clearest single case of it.",
